{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::execute` receives a context for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node` and computes its\ncompleted answer:\n\n```rust,ignore\nuse std::future::Future;\n\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Select inputs through cx and compute the completed answer.\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        cx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAn `Ok` value is the answer defined by\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait.answer`.\nAn `Err` value reports a [validation error](running-criteria.md#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ValidationError).",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.execute` is the prescribed `Criterion::execute` signature. It takes `self` by value. It takes a `&mut ValidationContext<'_, D>`. It returns `impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send`. Changing the receiver, the context type or the return type would violate the clause even if the runtime results stayed the same. The root `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#trait[Criterion]/fn[execute]` declares exactly this signature and carries the {impl} marker. The only difference is the parameter name `ctx` instead of `cx`, which is cosmetic. The remaining clauses are behavioral: that `Ok` is the answer defined by `criterion_trait.answer`, and that `Err` reports a validation error. Those need no further structural evidence. `Validator::execute` in the same file is a caller. It is not a trait declaration, so it needs no marker.\n\nbehavioral: I judged this from the supplied test sources only. I did not read implementation source or the elided parts of the tests. The requirement `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.execute` has four testable obligations, and each has marked tests.\n\n1. **`execute` runs for a source node's context and computes the completed answer in `Ok`.**\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` runs a deterministic criterion through `Validator::verify`. It asserts the result's `output().result` and `rationale`. It also asserts that the saved dependency is `Dependency::Node(Node(1), …)` with the fingerprint \"ordinary computation\".\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls]` checks that `execute` can select inputs through the context. It asserts the dependency on node 1, the intermediate agent request, and the resulting answer.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync]` asserts output values of 17 and 23, and checks that execution runs once or twice as expected.\n\n2. **An `Ok` value is the completed answer, including a negative one.** `criterion_decode_errors_are_execution_errors_and_false_is_a_completed_answer` has a criterion return `false`. It asserts that `verify` returns `Ok`, that the output is false, that the dependency is recorded, and that the stored record's output is `json!(false)`.\n\n3. **An `Err` value reports a `ValidationError`.**\n   - `criterion_decode_errors_are_execution_errors_and_false_is_a_completed_answer` shows a decode failure from `execute` surfacing as `ValidationError::Output`, with no record stored.\n   - `runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls` shows a missing agent surfacing as `NoAgentConfigured`, with no records and no reads stored.\n   - `resolution_storage_and_callback_errors_are_not_judgments` shows a graph error raised in a callback surfacing as `Graph(\"missing\")`, with no record stored.\n   - `ending_without_a_corrected_submission_is_an_agent_error` shows an agent failure surfacing as `ValidationError::Agent`, with no record stored.\n   - `agent_errors_expose_the_agreed_string_payload` checks the error payload shape.\n\n4. **The returned future is `Send` and takes `&mut ValidationContext`.** `validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync` wraps the `verify` future in `send(...)`, which checks that it is `Send`.\n\nThe tests cover both success and error paths, with assertions on each outcome. Every relevant test already carries a `{test}` reference to the subject, so I found no gap and no marking gap.",
  "references": "0c4c45be2d4110ca0050b9a7765a8ed96856c7f2bc304ddac82da02603c5d9b7",
